From f123946249f846a40fe8a959fdc1c397513b6a1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kernel86-64 <87580651+kernel86-64@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:01:59 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 33/57] drm/msm/dpu: validate bandwidth against the proposed
 atomic state

The bandwidth check reads new_perf from the currently installed CRTC state. A composition can therefore exceed the limit at commit time and leave subsequent commits, including a cheaper client-composition fallback, stuck returning -E2BIG. On joan this was observed with a 7023315 kB/s vote against a 6700000 kB/s limit.

Calculate the proposed CRTC performance from visible planes and their assigned pipes during atomic check. Once all proposed CRTC costs are available, check the combined bandwidth, taking the modeset locks for unaffected CRTCs without adding them to the commit. Preserve the bandwidth limit and let userspace fall back to a composition that fits.

The older joan kernel diagnosed the same stale-state check in 713905be235ea (drm/msm/dpu: check bandwidth against the proposed atomic state). Linux 7.3 finalizes split pipe geometry during CRTC resource assignment and can use more than two pipes, so account for all configured pipes after assignment.

Signed-off-by: kernel86-64 <87580651+kernel86-64@users.noreply.github.com>
